  "name": "Question 18",
  "problem": "Let $x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ k \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].$ Find $k,$ if $‖x‖=3\\sqrt{6}.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we calculate the norm of the vector $x$ in terms of $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖x‖ & =\\sqrt{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}+{x}_{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{k}^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+9+{k}^{2}+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{k}^{2}+29}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given equation  $‖x‖=3\\sqrt{6},$ we obtain an equation that we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{k}^{2}+29} & =3\\sqrt{6} \\\\ {(\\sqrt{{k}^{2}+29})}^{2} & ={(3\\sqrt{6})}^{2} \\\\ {k}^{2}+29 & =9\\cdot 6 \\\\ {k}^{2}+29 & =54 \\\\ {k}^{2} & =25 \\\\ k & =\\pm 5\\end{aligned}\n$$",
